<TopicPage slug="subjects/visual-arts/advanced/figure-drawing-and-anatomy">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Figure drawing is the classical atelier\'s summit — <strong>gesture, proportion, anatomy and
    likeness layered in order</strong>. It is the most codified discipline in art education
    because the subject is the most demanding judge: everyone alive is an expert in what humans
    look like.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="gesture, canon, armature, the hard parts" widget="equationStep" config={figureCfg} />

  <h2>The living tradition</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Life drawing sessions are the gym:</strong> timed poses from 1 minute to 3 hours — short poses train gesture and decision, long poses train structure and patience; both weekly, for years, is how the skill compounds (the training lesson\'s supercomplication, in charcoal).</li>
    <li><strong>Anatomy from function:</strong> learn muscles by what they DO (the biceps flexes the elbow — it bulges when you test it) — functional anatomy sticks where noun-lists evaporate (the biology lesson\'s muscle chapter, artistically abridged).</li>
    <li><strong>Portrait likeness is proportional deviation:</strong> not "eyes nose mouth" but the half-millimetre differences — measuring (the observation lesson) is how you find them; every portrait class is a measurement class in disguise.</li>
    <li><strong>Drapery is figure drawing\'s exam:</strong> the folds wrap the forms beneath (tension, compression, gravity points) — Michelangelo\'s figures read partly through sculpted cloth.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Bridgman, Constructive Anatomy; Hale, Drawing Lessons from the Great Masters; the anatomy of the medicine ladder, abridged.</p>
</TopicPage>
